# 50% sampling robustness and strong baseline v1

## Hypothesis

The marginal 50% reservoir result is driven in part by random label-composition
and timing variation.  A proportional label-stratified 50% sample should be a
stronger simple supervised baseline, but neither sampler is considered locally
promising unless it passes a fixed seed-robust aggregate gate.

## Frozen protocol

- Same cached native train/test split, metadata removal, train-only TF-IDF, and
  sparse Ridge objective as `discriminative_compression_pilot_v1`.
- Repeat the deterministic full sparse baseline three times in the same warm
  process.  Use its median wall time as the time denominator and verify metrics
  are bitwise identical across repeats.
- Sample exactly round(0.5 * 11,314) training documents.
- New seeds: 307, 401, 503, 601, 701.
- `uniform_reservoir`: the same one-pass uniform reservoir algorithm.
- `stratified_uniform`: `StratifiedShuffleSplit`, using labels only to preserve
  class proportions; no feature or test-label information is used.
- Each sample fits its own vectorizer and classifier and transforms the native
  test set once.  Count selection, vectorization, fit, and prediction.

## Aggregate decision rule

A sampler is locally promising only if, among the five new seeds:

1. median macro-F1 loss relative to repeated full sparse is at most 0.03;
2. at least four of five seeds individually have loss at most 0.03;
3. median wall-time ratio is at most 0.75; and
4. every sample retains all 20 classes.

This is a robustness screen, not a probability guarantee.

## Predictions and stop rule

Uniform reservoir is expected to remain near the quality boundary and may fail
the four-of-five rule.  Stratification should reduce class-count variation; if
it passes, it becomes the strong simple example-compression baseline for any
later learned coreset.  If neither passes, close batch example compression on
this benchmark.  Regardless of outcome, do not revisit SVD-128/256, which the
pilot already showed to be dominated.

